SLIDE 2 — IDEA TITLE

Proposed Solution

Agentic vision-language assistant: upload 1 image, a bi-temporal pair, or an optical+SAR pair — ask in plain English, get an evidence-grounded answer
PaliGemma-3B QLoRA-fine-tuned on RSVQA-LR (772 Sentinel-2 scenes, balanced subset) — a domain-adapted VLM, not a generic API call
Controller classifies intent → validates geospatial inputs → selects & sequences specialist tools → merges text + spatial evidence
5 tasks: single-image VQA, scene captioning, region grounding, bi-temporal change analysis, optical–SAR fusion

How it addresses the problem

Non-experts need no GIS skills, no model selection, no task parameters — one text box
Native GeoTIFF / multispectral / SAR ingestion: 12-band Sentinel-2, single-band float32 radar, dB conversion, Lee speckle filtering
Paired tasks run a deterministic spatial tool + the VLM, then merge: the algorithm answers where, the VLM answers what
Every answer ships with visual overlay, typed confidence, audit trail, GeoJSON + HTML report

Innovation and uniqueness

Physics-based SAR extraction — specular return ⇒ water, double-bounce + high texture ⇒ built-up; optical cross-check rejects radar shadow. Not a generic model shown a grey picture
Anti-hallucination audit trail — every tool reports its real model ID and an explicit remote_sensing_adapted: true/false. Fallbacks are declared, never disguised
Zero-network operation — deterministic offline intent router; no API key required, no single point of failure on demo day
Anti-degeneracy gates — dataset builders and the trainer both abort if the model learns the answer prior instead of the imagery
SLIDE 3 — TECHNICAL APPROACH

Technologies to be used

VLM: google/paligemma-3b-pt-224 (SigLIP-400M vision + Gemma-2B), native <loc0000>–<loc1023> grounding tokens
Adaptation: QLoRA — 4-bit NF4, FP16 compute, rank 16 / alpha 32, gradient checkpointing; LoRA on LM attention + MLP, vision-tower attention
Datasets: RSVQA-LR (VQA) · VRSBench (captioning/grounding) · CDVQA (change) · BigEarthNet-MM (optical–SAR)
Geospatial: rasterio, GDAL — CRS transforms, reprojection, percentile stretch, Lee filter, Otsu, CVA
Stack: PyTorch · Transformers · PEFT · BitsAndBytes · NumPy · Streamlit · pytest
Hardware: RTX 5060 Ti 16 GB (training) · runs on CPU for inference

Methodology and process for implementation

Suggested diagram — horizontal flow, left to right:

Query + Image(s)
   ↓
INTENT ROUTER          Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite  ⇄  Offline rule-based (fallback)
   ↓
INPUT VALIDATOR        format · bands · CRS · co-registration (7 outcomes)
   ↓
AGENTIC CONTROLLER     select → sequence → execute → merge → confidence → trace
   ↓
   ├── VQA / Caption ──── PaliGemma-3B + LoRA (RSVQA)
   ├── Grounding ──────── GroundingDINO-tiny  [zero-shot, flagged not RS-adapted]
   ├── Change ─────────── CVA + Otsu + morphology  →  PaliGemma change adapter
   └── Optical–SAR ────── backscatter physics  →  PaliGemma (VQA adapter fallback)
   ↓
STREAMLIT UI           overlays · audit trail · GeoJSON · HTML report
Change pipeline: IQR radiometric normalisation → Change Vector Analysis → Otsu (data-driven, not hardcoded) → morphological open/close → connected components
Train/serve consistency: training and inference import the same compositing module — the model is never shown a rendering it did not train on
SLIDE 4 — FEASIBILITY AND VIABILITY

Analysis of the feasibility

Working end-to-end prototype today: Streamlit app, 5 tools, full agentic controller
3B model + QLoRA trains on one consumer GPU; inference runs on CPU — deployable on ISRO/SAC hardware without a cluster
Unit suite (~60 tests) covers the deterministic core — no GPU, no network, no weights required
All datasets are open and are the exact benchmarks named in the problem statement

Potential challenges and risks

SAR is out-of-distribution for an optically-trained VLM — the largest technical risk
Grounding currently falls back to zero-shot GroundingDINO — not remote-sensing adapted
Optical–SAR VLM path falls back to the VQA adapter; radar-derived claims flagged as lower confidence
Uncalibrated DN input prevents absolute σ⁰ thresholds
CPU latency for a 3B VLM

Strategies for overcoming these challenges

BigEarthNet-MM builder ready — real co-registered Sentinel-1 + Sentinel-2 for a dedicated fusion adapter; SAR rendered through the identical dB→Lee→stretch path used at inference
Grounding adapter trains PaliGemma's native detect head on VRSBench referring boxes — same pipeline, no separate detector
Fallbacks are never hidden: the trace records which backend ran, so a judge can verify every claim
SAR classified by scene-relative percentiles — robust to calibration state, and labelled as such
GPU inference for live demos; result cache surfaced explicitly with a cache_hit flag
SLIDE 5 — IMPACT AND BENEFITS

Potential impact on the target audience

Disaster response: flood extent from SAR through cloud cover, day or night, in minutes
Non-expert analysts: district officers, agriculture and forest departments query imagery without GIS training
ISRO/SAC analysts: one interface across Cartosat-2S optical and RISAT SAR instead of separate task-specific tools
Auditability: every output carries model IDs, applied parameters and confidence type — usable as an evidence record, not just a demo

Benefits of the solution

Social: faster flood/landslide assessment; democratised access to national satellite assets
Economic: open-weight 3B model, consumer-GPU training, CPU inference — no per-query API cost, no vendor lock-in, fully deployable inside ISRO's network
Environmental: deforestation, water-body shrinkage, urban-sprawl monitoring from bi-temporal pairs with quantified changed-area statistics
Strategic: sovereign AI capability — model weights, code and data pipeline are entirely in-house and reproducible
